    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kraków grew from a fortified settlement of the Vistulans on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel Hill</a> into the seat of the Polish government from 1038 and the capital of the kingdom until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> moved his court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1596.</li><li>The city was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1241, rebuilt from 1257 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights, and gained the university founded by Casimir III in 1364, which became a centre of Polish learning and art under the Jagiellonian kings.</li><li>After the partitions it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, became a Polish national and cultural centre under Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, and was the capital of the General Government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when its large Jewish population was confined in a ghetto and murdered.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The so-called dragon bones displayed at the entrance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> Cathedral, tied to the legend of the dragon of Wawel Hill, are most likely those of a mammoth.</p></aside>
    </section>

