    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8th century</span><span class="rf-text">Vistulans found a fortified settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel Hill</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1038</span><span class="rf-text">Kraków becomes the seat of the Polish government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1241</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sack and burn the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1364</span><span class="rf-text">Casimir III founds the university, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Jagiellonian University</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1596</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> moves the royal court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1795</span><span class="rf-text">After the third partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the city passes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1939</span><span class="rf-text">Kraków becomes the capital of the General Government under Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1978</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> lists the Old Town and Karol Wojtyła, archbishop of Kraków, becomes pope</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wawel-hill.jpg" alt="Wawel Hill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel Hill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wawel_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Monika Towiańska, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kraków stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, about 219 metres above sea level, where several physiographic regions meet: the Kraków-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a> Upland in the north-west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Małopolska</a> Upland in the north-east, the Sandomierz Basin in the east and the Western Beskidian Foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> in the south. The earliest evidence of human settlement comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> Castle. The city began as a fortified settlement of the Vistulans, a Lechitic tribe, around the 8th century.</p><p>A legend attributes the founding to the mythical ruler Krakus, who built the town above a cave occupied by a dragon, Smok Wawelski. Many knights failed to drive the dragon out, but Krakus fed it a poisoned lamb, which killed it. The name is traditionally derived from Krakus, a ruler of the Vistulans; its true origin is highly disputed among historians, and it may come from the word kruk, meaning crow or raven. Krakus is first recorded, as Grakch, in 1190.</p><p>Before the Polish state formed, Kraków was the capital of the Vistulans. They were subjugated for a short period by Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, and the city then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, which held it until the Piast ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> took it towards the end of his reign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste.jpg" alt="Bolesław V the Chaste" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste">Bolesław V the Chaste</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piecz%C4%99%C4%87_Boles%C5%82awa_Wstydliwego.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Zuber at Polish Wikipedia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the end of the 10th century Kraków was a leading centre of trade. Brick buildings went up, among them the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> Castle with the rotunda of St Felix and Adauctus, Romanesque churches such as St Andrew's, a cathedral and a basilica. Its population doubled from 5,000 to 10,000 between 1100 and 1300 and stood at 14,000 in 1400, and by 1550 the metropolitan area had 18,000 inhabitants.</p><p>In 1257 the city was rebuilt after the Mongol attacks on a new location act, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste">Bolesław V the Chaste</a> gave it city rights modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights, with tax benefits and new trade privileges. Medieval Kraków was surrounded by a defensive wall of about 3 km with 46 towers and seven main entrances, built over two centuries. In 1335 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> declared two western suburbs a new city, Kazimierz, and in 1364 he founded the university.</p><p>The city continued to grow under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>. As the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> it drew craftsmen, businesses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> from abroad. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> reached it in the late 15th century with Italian artists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veit-stoss">Veit Stoss</a> finished the high altar of St Mary's Church in 1489, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> the Wawel Castle was remade as a Renaissance palace.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus.jpg" alt="Sigismund II Augustus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cranach_the_Younger_Sigismund_II_Augustus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kraków was the seat of the Vistulans, then briefly held by Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, and a Bohemian duke, Boleslaus I, took it in 955. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, the first acclaimed ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, took it from the Bohemians and added it to the Piast holdings. In 1038 it became the seat of the Polish government. The Testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a>, which took effect in 1138, divided Poland into five provinces and named Kraków the Seniorate Province, to be ruled by the eldest male of the royal family as High Duke. Infighting among his descendants followed, and the fragmentation of Poland lasted until 1320.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a> reigned from 1386 to 1572. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a>, the last of the Jagiellons, died childless in 1572, the throne passed to Henry III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and then to other foreign-based rulers in quick succession, and the city's importance declined. In 1596 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> moved the administrative capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p>After the partitions Kraków fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made it part of the Duchy of Warsaw in 1809, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 created the Free City of Cracow. After the uprising of 1846 it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> as the Grand Duchy of Kraków, within Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. Austrian rule ended in 1918, and Kraków joined the Second Polish Republic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus.jpg" alt="Nicolaus Copernicus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikolaus_Kopernikus_MOT.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city's population was mixed from an early date. By the end of the 13th century German-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> formed the majority of its magistrates and merchants. After the rebellion of the wójt Albert in 1311, in which many of them were massacred or fled, the burgher class was gradually re-Polonised. The city's law, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>' depictions and descriptions, was recorded in German in the Balthasar Behem Codex. A cathedral school had existed since 1150, and after 1364 the royal chancery and the university brought a first flowering of Polish literary culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> studied at the Academy, and Johann Haller had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in the city by 1500.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in Kraków from the early 12th century. In 1495 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> I Albert expelled them from within the city walls, and they moved to Kazimierz, where in 1553 the Jewish community received a licence to build its own interior walls. Synagogues such as the Old Synagogue were built there. Between the wars Kraków was an important centre for Polish Jews, with Zionist and Bundist groups and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Hasidic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-judaism">Reform Judaism</a> side by side. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1931, 78.1% of the city's people declared Polish as their primary language and 20.9% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>.</p><p>The Main Market Square, the largest medieval market square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, lies at the centre of the Old Town. A trumpet call is sounded every hour from St Mary's Basilica and breaks off in mid-tune; according to legend it recalls a guard shot by a Mongol archer.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi.jpg" alt="George II Rákóczi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi">George II Rákóczi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I.r%C3%A1k%C3%B3czi_gy%C3%B6rgy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked and burned Kraków in 1241, and it was almost entirely destroyed. They ravaged it again in 1259, and a third attack in 1287 was repelled in part thanks to the new fortifications. A fire in 1494 was followed by a wave of anti-Jewish attacks. In the Swedish invasion the city was besieged and captured in 1655, and in December 1656 the Transylvanian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi">George II Rákóczi</a>, an ally of the Swedish king, occupied it. An outbreak of bubonic plague in 1707 left 20,000 of its residents dead.</p><p>In 1794 the Prussian army took the city and looted the royal treasure kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> Castle; the regalia, valued at 525,259 thalers, were secretly melted down in March 1809. The Kraków Uprising of 1846 was crushed by the Austrian authorities, and in 1850 a large fire destroyed about 10% of the city.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in November 1939, the Germans arrested more than 180 university professors and academics and sent them to the Sachsenhausen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dachau-concentration-camp">Dachau concentration camps</a>; the survivors were released after protests. In March 1941 the Germans designated the suburb of Podgórze as the Kraków Ghetto, where many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> died of illness or starvation. The largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> came from June to September 1942, with mass deportation from the ghetto in the first week of June, and inhabitants were sent to Bełżec, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> and the Płaszów camp. About 56,000 Jews had lived in the city before the war, almost a quarter of its population of about 250,000. After the war about 5,900 remained. The city was looted but remained relatively undamaged.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/veit-stoss"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veit-stoss.jpg" alt="Veit Stoss" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/veit-stoss">Veit Stoss</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veit_Sto%C3%9F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Hoss at German Wikipedia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old Town and the historic centre of Kraków, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> Castle and Cathedral, the Main Market Square, the Cloth Hall, St Florian's Gate and the Barbican and Kazimierz, were placed on the first list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in 1978. Wawel Cathedral holds the burials of many Polish kings. St Mary's Basilica has the altarpiece by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veit-stoss">Veit Stoss</a>, the largest Gothic altarpiece in the world. The Planty Park, laid out between 1822 and 1830, took the place of the old city walls. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Jagiellonian University</a>, founded in 1364, is the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the Jagiellonian Library holds more than four million volumes, among them Copernicus's De Revolutionibus.</p><p>Under Austrian rule after 1866 Kraków became a Polish national symbol and a centre of culture and art, known as the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Painters and writers such as Jan Matejko and Stanisław Wyspiański lived there. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Polish People's Republic built the country's largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> mill in the new suburb of Nowa Huta, which turned Kraków from a university city into an industrial centre. Karol Wojtyła, archbishop of Kraków from 1964 to 1978, was elevated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">John Paul II</a> on 16 October 1978, the first non-Italian pope in 455 years.</p><p>Kraków is today the second-largest city of Poland and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> Voivodeship, with about 816,000 people.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kraków, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, began as a fortified settlement around the 8th century and was the seat of Polish monarchs from 1038 until the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1596. It kept its university and cultural life under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule and survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> largely undamaged.</em></p>

